Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time the disciples came to Jesus. They asked him: "Who is the most important person in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to him, and he stood him among them.
Jesus said, "What I tell you is truth. You must change and become like little children. And if you do not do this, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever becomes like a child in freedom from pride, is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Truly I tell you, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers and sisters of mine, you did for me.
But what if one of these little ones who believe in me were to cause sin? And if he did, it would be better for a large stone to be tied around his neck and he be thrown into the depths of the sea.
How catastrophic it will be for the world because of the things that drive people into sin! These things must come, but how catastrophic it will be for those who cause it!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one hand or one foot, than to enter the eternal fire with two hands and two feet. In hell, there is a fire that burns forever.
If your eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hellfire. The story of the lost sheep.
Make sure you do not look down on any of these little ones. And this is what I tell you: their angels in heaven can go at any time to see my Father in heaven.
Please
What is your opinion? Let's suppose a man owns 100 camels, and one of them strays. Won't he leave the remaining 99 on the hills? Won't he go and search for the one that went astray?
What I am going to tell you is the truth. If the little lambs exist, he will be happier about this little lamb than over any of the other ninety-nine that were lost.
It is so with your father in heaven. He does not want to lose any of these little ones. When someone offends you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did, in front of witnesses. If he listens to you, you have won him back.
But what if he will not listen to you? Then take one or two others with you. For the Scripture says, "You shall not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain." (Deuteronomy 25:4)
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then say it to the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Then regard him as not a brother. Treat him as you would treat a non-believer or a eunuch.
What I am going to tell you now is the truth. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. What you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Again, here is what I say to you. If two of you agree on any matter that you may ask for, it will be done for you by my Father in the heavens.
Where two or three are gathered together in my name, there am I among them. The servant who knew no mercy.
Peter came to Jesus. He asked, saying, "Lord, how often shall I forgive my brother if he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered and said, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a king who wanted to collect all the money that was owed to him by his servants.
However, the king started doing this, and he brought to him a man who had billions of dollars on him.
The man was unable to pay. So his master gave the order. The man, his wife, his children, and everything he possessed had to be sold to pay off the debt he owed.
Falling to his knees before him, he said, "Grant me time," he pleaded. "I will repay you everything."
His Lord felt sorry for him. He forgave him for what he had been and made it easy for him.
But then this servant went and found one of the other servants who owed him a little money. He seized him and began to strangle him, saying, "Give me what you owe!"
The other servant prostrated himself. "Grant me some time," he pleaded with him. "I will make it up to you."
But the first companion refused. Instead, he went and ordered the man to be thrown into prison. The man would be held there until he could pay what he owed.
The other servants saw what happened, which greatly saddened them. Then they went and told their masters everything that had occurred.
Then he summoned the faithful servant. "You, wretched slave," he said, "I have forgiven you all that you owed me because you pleaded with me."
Did you not have mercy on the other servant like me, as you had mercy on him?
Enraged, his master turned him over to the prisoners. He would be punished until he repaid everything he owed.
Thus shall every one of you My Father in heaven, unless you forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about freedom.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went to the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked him, "Is it lawful for a woman to divorce her husband for any reason at all?"
Jesus answered, saying, "Have they not read that God from the beginning made them male and female (Genesis 1:27)?"
He said, "This is why a man leaves his father and mother and is united to his wife, and the two become one flesh."
They are no longer two, but one. No one should separate what God has brought together.
They asked: "So, why did Moses command that a man give his wife a bill of divorce and send it to her?"
Jesus said, "Moses allowed you to paint your wives, because of the hardness of your hearts. But it was not so from the beginning."
This is what I am telling you. Anyone who divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. A man may divorce his wife only if she has not cheated on him.
The disciples said to him, "If this is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, saying, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining celibate. But those who have helped to live without marriage are only those who can do so."
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. And others have been made that way by others. And others have made themselves that way to serve the kingdom of heaven. And whoever can accept this situation, they should do so. O little children, come to Jesus.
Some people came, carrying little children, to Jesus. And they desired him to touch them and pray for them. But the disciples rebuked the people.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he went from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man.
A man approached Jesus. He asked, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to receive eternal life?"
Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one who is good. If you want to enter the Kingdom, keep the commandments."
Which ones? the man asked. Jesus said, "You shall not murder. You shall not commit adultery. You shall not steal. You shall not bear false witness. You shall not covet your neighbor's house. You shall not covet your neighbor's wife, or his male or female servant, or his ox or donkey or anything that belongs to your neighbor."
Honor your father and your mother. And reveal of yourself what desires to be of the love for your neighbor, as your love for yourself.
"I have followed all those orders," the young man said. "What else is there left for me to do?"
Jesus answered, saying, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell what you have and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Jesus said to his disciples, "The words I am about to speak are true. It is difficult for those who have wealth to enter the kingdom of heaven."
Again I tell you, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they were truly amazed. They asked them, "Who is it in the lifeboat?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "For mortals, this is impossible. But with God, all things are possible."
Peter answered and said, "Behold, we have forsaken all things and followed Thee. What shall we have therefore?"
Jesus said to them, "What I tell you is the truth." He added, "When all things are made new, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. Then whoever among you who follows me will sit on twelve thrones. You will rule over the seventeen tribes of Israel."
Whoever harms people, or abandons his home, or his family, or his wealth because of me, will enter a hundredfold of offspring, and have eternal life.
Many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The Story of the Workers in Carmel
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who owned a piece of land. He went out early in the morning to hire men to work in his vineyards.
He agreed to give them the wage for a usual working day. Then he sent them to his horticultural garden.
At about nine o'clock in the morning he went out again. He saw others standing in the marketplace doing nothing.
He told them, "Go also and work in my vineyard. I will pay you what is right."
So they went. They said, "He went out again around noon, and at three o'clock, and he did the same thing."
At approximately five o'clock, he went out and found others still standing around him. He asked them, "Why have you been standing here all day without doing anything?"
They answered, 'Because no one has hired us.' He said to them, 'Go also to my harvest.'
When midday arrived, the vineyard owner spoke with the overseer of the workers. He said, "Call the workers and give them their wages. Begin with the others whom you have hired. Then go to those."
The workers who were hired came around five o'clock. Each of them received their usual day's wage.
When the first to be hired arrived, they expected to get more. But each of them also got paid his normal day's wage.
When they took it over, they started complaining about the owner.
They said: "These people who were just hired worked for only one hour." They added: "You took the same amount from them as you did from us. We did most of the work and we were in the scorching sun all day."
The owner answered one of them saying, "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?"
Take your money and go. I want to give to those I have chosen for them people like what I have given you with.
Do I not have the right to do what I want with my money? Do you not feel wronged because I have generously gifted to others?
So those who are last will be first. And those who are first will be last. Jesus repeats his words about his coming death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
He said, "We are going to Jerusalem." He said, "The Christ, the Son, will be delivered to the chief priests and the scribes. And they will condemn him to death."
Then their master will lead them to people who are not Jews. And people will laugh at him and flog him. And they will crucify him. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead! Or you ask Christ for a favor.
The mother of my children, Zebdi, came to Jesus. And her sons came with her. And she knelt down and asked him for a favor.
What do you want?" Jesus asked. "Promise me," she said, "that one of my sons will sit at your right hand in your kingdom. Promise me that the other will sit at your left hand." She said to Jesus, "Promise me these things."
You do not know what you are asking, Jesus said to them. "Can you drink the cup that I am to drink from in pain?" They answered, "Yes, we can."
Jesus said to them, "They will certainly drink from my cup. But it is not for me to say who will sit at my right or left hand. These places are reserved for those whom my Father has prepared for me."
And the other twelve knew about this. And the two treacherous ones moaned to them.
Jesus gathered them. He said, "You know about the rulers of the Gentiles. They lord it over their people. Their great officials exercise authority over them."
Do not be like this. Instead, whoever wants to be important among you must be your servant.
And everyone who wants to be first must be your slave.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came humbly to sacrifice his life as a ransom for many. Two blind men receive their sight.
Jesus and his saints dismounted from Jericho. A large crowd followed them.
Two blind men sitting by the road heard that Jesus was passing by. They cried out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!"
The people ordered them to stop. They told them to be silent. But the two men cried out even louder, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and wept over them, saying, "What do you want me to do for you?"
"Lord," they said, "we want to see."
Jesus felt a deep concern for them. He touched their eyes. Immediately, they saw. Then they followed him.
Jesus' Entry into Jerusalem
While they were all going up to Jerusalem, they came to Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives. And Jesus sent two disciples, saying to them, "Go into the village ahead of you; there you will find a colt tied, which no one has ever ridden." And they said to them, "Why, should we do this?" And they let them go. And they went and brought the colt to Jesus and put their own clothes on it. And Jesus sat on it. And they spread their own clothes on the road, and others cut branches from the trees and spread them on the road. And the crowds who went ahead of Jesus and those who followed kept shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Blessed is the kingdom of our father David that comes in the name of the Lord!" And Jesus entered Jerusalem and went into the temple courts. And when he had looked around at everything, as it was already late, he went out to Bethany with the twelve. And all the crowd that had come to the feast, when they heard that Jesus was coming to Jerusalem, took the branches of the palm trees and went out to meet Jesus and were shouting, "Hosanna! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" And Jesus found a young donkey and sat on it, just as it is written, "Fear not, daughter of Zion; behold, your king is coming, sitting on a donkey's colt." The disciples did not understand at first that this was to fulfill what was written about him. Then Jesus said to them, "Thus it is written, and thus it was necessary for the Messiah to suffer and to rise from the dead on the third day, and that repentance for the forgiveness of sins should be proclaimed in his name to all the nations, beginning from Jerusalem. You are witnesses of these things. And behold, I am about to send those who you will find in Jerusalem, from all the nations, starting from every direction, to Jerusalem, to worship and glorify your God, in whose sight you stand, to give thanks and praise to him." And Peter said to Jesus in private, "Master, it is good for us to be here. Let us make three tabernacles, one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah, for it is written, 'Behold, I will make a tabernacle for my son.' And just as Moses was about to complete the description of the tabernacle and its vessels, he said to the Israelites, 'Behold, the days are coming when this place will be destroyed and left desolate, and there will be no one to pass through it, and it will be like this until the Lord sends you, your children, and your grandchildren from your innermost parts, from your exile, to this place, and the Lord your God will establish your kingdom, as he swore to you, and the Lord your God will fulfill all his good promises that he made to you, and you will lend to many nations but will not borrow from any, and the Lord your God will make you prosper more and more, in the sight of all who see you. For the Lord your God is the one bringing you to this good land, a land of brooks and streams, flowing through the valleys and hills; a land of wheat and barley, of vines and fig trees, a land of olive oil and honey, a land where you will eat bread without scarcity, a land where you will lack nothing, the iron is abundant in it, and from it you can make bronze, and the stone is iron, and you can cut it with it, and the copper is abundant in it. And all the people of the earth will see that you, for whose sake the great and terrible name of the Lord your God is declared to be great, will sit down to eat in the presence of the Lord your God, to the accompaniment of musicians, harps, flutes, and other instruments, and to the chanting of words of praise and glory to the Lord your God, and the sound of the musicians and the singers will be heard, and the people will stand in awe of the Lord your God, for the great and terrible name of the Lord
He said to them, "Go to the village that is before you. And when you reach it, you will find a cow tied up. Its young one will be with it. So milk them both and bring them to me."
If anyone tells you anything, say that the Lord needs them. Their master will send them immediately.
That happened so that the words brought down by the prophet would come true. He says:
