When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city shook. The people asked, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus. He is the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus purifies the temple.
Jesus entered the temple area. He began to drive out those who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of those who were exchanging money. He also overturned the seats of those who were selling doves.
He said to them: "It is written that the Lord has said, 'My house shall be called a house of prayer.' (Isaiah 56:7) But you are making it into a 'den of robbers.'" (Jeremiah 7:11)"
The deaf and the dumb, and others of the physically impaired, came to Jesus at the mosque, and there he restored them to health.
The worthy priests and religious leaders saw the wonderful things he was doing. They also saw the children in the temple area shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" But when they saw all this, they became angry.
Do you hear what these children are saying? 'They have made you a decision.' 'Yes,' Jesus answered. 'Have you not read in the Scriptures? It says, "I have made you a delight to children and to those who nurse infants."' (Psalm 8:2)
Then Jesus went out from the midst of the people, and went away from the city to Bethany. And He spent the night there. Being in an olive grove, He fell to the ground.
Early in the morning, Jesus was on his way back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a grapevine by the side of the road. He climbed up to it, but he found nothing on it except leaves. Then he said to it, "O God, do not be bitter fruit again!" And the tree was immediately transformed.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed. They said, "How did the yellow tree dry up so quickly?"
Jesus said, "The words I speak to you are spirit and truth. You must believe and not doubt, and in this way, you will perform the miracles you did with the fig tree. And you will say to this mountain, 'Go, fall into the sea.' And it will be done."
If you believe, you will be given what you ask for when you pray. Jesus' authority is questionable.
Jesus entered the temple courts. As he taught there, the chief priests and the teachers of the law came to him. "By what authority are you doing these things?" they asked. "Who gave you this authority?"
Jesus said, "I will also ask you one question more. If you answer me, I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From where was the hero of John conducted? Was it from heaven? Or from among men? They spoke to one another about it. They said, "If we say 'from heaven,' he will ask, 'Why did you not believe in him?'"
But what if we say 'of the men'? So we fear people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet.
They answered him, "Jesus, we do not know." Jesus said, "Then I will also not tell you by what authority I do these things." The Parable of the Two Sons.
What do you think about this? A man had two sons. He went to the first son and said to him, "My son, go and work today in the vineyards."
"I will not do that," the son answered. But then he changed his mind and went off.
Then the father went to the other son. He said the same thing to himself. And the son replied, "I will, sir." But he did not go.
Which son of the two shaykhs has done what his father will be angry about?" "The first one," they said. Yahya said to them, "What can I say to you? This is true. The Godfearing and the immoral will both enter the kingdom of heaven before you."
John came to show you how to live right. And you didn't believe him. But the poor and prostitutes believed him. And you saw that. But you didn't look at your own sins until then and you didn't believe him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. A man owned some land, so he planted vineyards. He put a fence around them. He dug a well for the inn to water it with. He also built a watchtower. He rented the vineyards to some peasants. Then he set off on a journey.
As the harvest approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. He instructed the servants to collect his share of the fruit.
But the tenants seized his slaves. They beat one of them. And they killed another one of them. And they threw stones at the third one to kill him.
Then the man sent two more servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at the first. And the tenants treated them in the same way.
Finally, he sent his son to them. He said, "My son will respect them."
But the tenants saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will get all the owner's property someday. Come, let's get rid of him. Then everything will be ours."
They seized him and threw him outside the Carmel. Then they killed him.
When the vineyard owner returns, what will he do to those he rents to?
They said, "These wicked ones will be destroyed." Then he will let out the vineyards to other tenants, and they will pay him his share of the fruit at the time of the harvest.
Jesus said to them, "Has not one of you read what the Scriptures say, 'The stone the builders rejected has become the capstone of the corner. This was the Lord's doing, and it is marvelous in our eyes'? (Psalm 118: 22, 23)"
Here is what I say to you. The kingdom of God will be taken from you. And it will be given to those who, when they have done their duty, seek its fruit.
He who falls upon that rock will be broken to pieces. But the rock will give a bitter taste to anyone who falls upon it.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They learned that he was speaking about them.
They looked for a way to arrest him. But they were afraid of the crowd. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
A Tale of a Wedding Dinner
Then Jesus told them more parables. He said
This is what the kingdom of heaven is like. A king who ruled a distant country prepared a wedding feast for his son.
He sent his servants to the guests invited to the dinner. And he told them to come, but they refused.
Then he sent other servants. He said, "Tell the invited guests that I have prepared the dinner. I have killed my oxen and fattened pigs. Everything is ready. Come to the wedding feast."
But people did not give the matter any attention. One of them went to his field, and another went to his work.
The others captured his servers. And they treated them badly and then killed them.
The king became very angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed these murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The wedding feast is ready. But those I invited were not ready to come."
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you find.
So the slaves went out into the streets. They gathered together all the people that they found, whether they were good or bad. And very soon the Hall of Supporters was filled with guests.
The king came to see the guests. He noticed a man among them who was not wearing wedding clothes.
My friend, he asked, "How did you get in here without the wedding clothes?" The man could not think of anything to say.
Then the king said to his servants, "Remove from him hands and feet. Guard him outside into the darkness. There they will pour, weep and sigh."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and plotted how they might entangle Jesus in His words.
Send their followers to him. And they sent the Hورديين with them. They said, "Our teacher, we know that you are a man of honor. You know the way of God sincerely. Do not allow others to tell you what to do or say. Do not care who is more important."
Tell us, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar, or not?
And Jesus, seeking to reveal their evil schemes, said, "Rebels! Why are you trying to trap me?"
Show me the money they use to pay taxes. They brought him a silver coin.
He asked them: "Who is in this picture? And who are these words?"
"Cassius," they answered. Then he said to them, "Give to Cassius his due, and give to God His due."
When they heard this, they were amazed. So they left him and went away. Marriage, when the dead rise.
On the same day, the Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe in their resurrection from the dead.
"O teacher, here is what Moses said to us. If a man dies and does not leave an heir, then he must marry the widow. He must have children with her to carry on his name."
There were seven of us brothers. The first one got married and died. Since he did not leave an heir, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second and third brothers. It continued until the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Well, when the dead rise, who will be his wife? They were all seven married to her.
Jesus said, "You are wrong, because you do not know the scriptures, nor the power of God."
When they lie from the dead, they will not marry. And their parents will not give them to marry. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the dead that are resurrected? Have you not heard what God has said to you?
He said: "I am God to whom they prayed and sought. I am God to whom Isaac offered his obedience. And I am God to whom Moses spoke. God is not for the spirits, but God is for the living."
When the horse heard this, they were able to teach him as much as they were able. The greatest matter.
