A man who was suffering from a skin disease came to Jesus. On his knees, he begged Jesus. "If you are willing," he said, "to make me 'clean,' then you can."
Jesus was filled with deep profound anxiety. He reached out and touched the man. He said, "I am ready to do that! Be 'clean'!"
Immediately, the disease left his body. He was cured.
Jesus immediately sent his servant away. And he gave the man a strong warning.
He said, "Don't tell this to anyone. Take yourself to the priest, and take the expiation that Moses commanded. He will be a witness to the priest and to the people that you are a 'naasef'."
But the man went out and began to talk at once. So he spread the news everywhere. And Jesus could no longer enter a town openly. But he remained in secluded places. Yet people still came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered Cana again. The people heard that he had returned to his house.
A large crowd of people gathered until there was no more room, not even outside the door. And Jesus spoke the word to them.
Four of those who came to them were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they could not get close to him, to Jesus, because of the crowd. So they made a hole in the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man on a stretcher through the hole.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some of the teachers of the law were sitting there, thinking.
Why does this man speak in this way? He is saying something extremely evil! Only God can forgive sins!
Immediately Jesus knew in his spirit what they were thinking in their hearts, and he said to them, "Why are you thinking these things in your hearts?
Is it easier for me to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Rise, take your mat and walk"?
I would like you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
